Phase 3 - Build the race catalog and compound predicates
    ☐ Add a dedicated race catalog module rather than embedding creature-raw policy in descriptors.lua or search.lua.
    ☐ Generate individual descriptors with stable IDs in the form race:raw:<CREATURE_ID>.
    ☐ Generate compound descriptors with IDs in the form race:group:<KEY>.
    ☐ Define ordered compounds for Humanoids, Trainable Animals, Domestic Animals, Wild Animals, Megabeasts, and Vermin.
    ☐ Derive compound membership from centralized creature/caste raw predicates and document the exact flags used by each group.
    ☐ Handle missing world raws, creature IDs, names, castes, and flags without crashing or accidentally matching a unit.
    ☐ Display compounds first in their defined order, then individual races alphabetically by display label with stable-ID tie-breaking.
    ☐ Keep citizen, resident, visitor, and hostile classifications out of the race catalog; they belong to unit scope.
    ☐ Reset raw-derived race metadata through the existing world/module lifecycle.
    Phase gate
        ☐ Catalog tests cover stable IDs, labels, ordering, duplicate protection, missing raws, and every compound predicate.
        ☐ Reordering the raw vector does not change a saved individual-race filter ID or its resolved creature.

Phase 4 - Implement the active race-filter provider and state invariants
    ☐ Add a race candidate provider that wraps any SoulSearchCandidateProvider.
    ☐ Resolve active race descriptors once per provider pass instead of once per unit.
    ☐ Union all positive race matches.
    ☐ Remove every negative race match after inclusion so exclusions always win.
    ☐ Preserve upstream order and suppress duplicates when compounds and individual races overlap.
    ☐ Make a new or first-loaded filter state default to include race:group:HUMANOIDS.
    ☐ Prevent remove, clear, replace, preset load, or stale-ID validation from leaving state without a positive race scope; restore Humanoids.
    ☐ Define Clear all as clearing ranking filters and resetting candidate filters to include Humanoids.
    ☐ Ensure negative-only external/preset input is normalized by adding positive Humanoids before applying exclusions.
    Phase gate
        ☐ Tests cover dwarves/elves/humans under Humanoids and dogs under animal groups.
        ☐ Tests cover multiple-positive union, overlapping groups, exclusions, all humanoids except dwarves, and trainable animals except dogs.
        ☐ Tests cover removal of the final positive, negative-only input, stale IDs, clear, replace, save/load, and preset load.

Phase 5 - Move candidate acquisition ahead of snapshots and ranking
    ☐ Separate resident/unit snapshot construction from candidate collection in residents.lua.
    ☐ Replace direct getCitizens() ownership with snapshot construction over units supplied by the provider chain.
    ☐ Make snapshots safe for animals and other units without souls, skills, translated names, professions, traits, or mental attributes.
    ☐ Preserve unit identity and live position access required by result selection and map zoom.
    ☐ Change UI refresh orchestration to build the provider chain, obtain candidates, snapshot them, then invoke search.apply.
    ☐ Pass only ranking filters to search.apply; do not add race branches to value evaluation, scoring, or result comparison.
    ☐ Recompute candidates and results whenever active race filters change.
    Phase gate
        ☐ Search tests prove race selection changes membership but never scoring or ordering among the surviving rows.
        ☐ Existing ranking characterization tests remain green without race-specific conditions in search.lua.
        ☐ Snapshot tests cover citizens, named pets, unnamed livestock, and units without souls.

Phase 6 - Add the Race filter UI
    ☐ Add a dedicated Add race filter control and picker alongside the Attribute and Skill controls.
    ☐ Add race picker state, query state, choices, refresh routing, close behavior, and mutual exclusion with every other picker.
    ☐ Show compound choices first and individual race choices afterward; search both by display label.
    ☐ Show a useful empty-search result when no race choice matches.
    ☐ Present race directions as Include and Exclude while retaining high and low in serialized state for compatibility.
    ☐ Reuse the existing direction-toggle and remove controls without changing their established hitboxes for ranking filters.
    ☐ Visually distinguish race filters as their own category.
    ☐ Do not offer ranking movement controls for race filters or count them as priority positions.
    ☐ When the last positive race is removed, immediately display the restored Humanoids filter and refreshed humanoid results.
    ☐ Update filter-panel help and tooltips to distinguish candidate scope from ranking criteria.
    Phase gate
        ☐ UI component, layout, formatting, refresh, tooltip, keyboard, and mouse tests cover the Race picker and active race rows.
        ☐ Attribute, Skill, and Preset picker behavior and existing filter-action hitboxes remain green.

Phase 7 - Presets, documentation, and compatibility
    ☐ Save candidate and ranking filters through the existing JSON preset format without changing the public entry shape.
    ☐ Load existing presets with no race entry by adding include Humanoids.
    ☐ Ignore stale individual-race IDs and restore Humanoids if no valid positive race remains.
    ☐ Verify custom, role, and skill presets preserve their ranking order while acquiring the default race scope.
    ☐ Update README usage and architecture sections for race filtering, candidate providers, include/exclude semantics, and default scope.
    ☐ Document the compound race definitions and the internal unit-scope choices for future scoped-window/settings work.
    ☐ Update docs/project.todo only after all automated and live acceptance gates pass.
    Phase gate
        ☐ Preset tests cover current JSON, legacy no-race presets, invalid race IDs, negative exclusions, and stable-ID round trips.
        ☐ Documentation describes implemented behavior and does not present internal unit-scope selection as user-facing.

